---
title: Hex frequencies
tags: tools
---
{% from "macros/tool.njk" import toolSection with context %}
{% from "macros/tool-frame.njk" import toolFrame %}
{% from "macros/delimiter.njk" import delimiter %}
{% set filename = page.fileSlug %}
{% set intro %}
<p class="px-2">
    For a given string of Hexadecimal, list out the frequencies of each byte, and of runs of the same byte repeated 2 to 5 times (such as <code class="d-inline-flex px-2 bg-success bg-opacity-10 border border-success border-opacity-10 rounded-2">00 00</code>). This can be useful when looking at data-driven puzzles, and trying to find recognizable patterns. Runs are counted wherever they start, and a longer run counts as several shorter ones: five <code class="d-inline-flex px-2 bg-success bg-opacity-10 border border-success border-opacity-10 rounded-2">41</code>s are two runs of 2.
</p>
<p class="px-2">
    Your hex can use any of the usual delimiters between bytes (spaces, hyphens, colons, commas, semicolons, new lines, \x, 0x or %), or none, and they can be mixed. The delimiter dropdown chooses how runs are written. Copy and download give the tables as tab-separated text, which pastes into a spreadsheet.
</p>
{% endset %}
{% call toolSection(intro) %}
{% call toolFrame(filename, "Hex frequencies", placeholder = "Example: 48 65 6c 6c 6f 00 00 00", layout = "analysis") %}
<button class="btn btn-convrtr" type="button" id="{{ filename }}Run">Count</button>
<div class="input-group w-auto">
    <label class="input-group-text" for="{{ filename }}Delimiter"><span class="visually-hidden">Hex </span>Delimiter</label>
    {{ delimiter(filename) }}
</div>
{% endcall %}
{% endcall %}
